ademy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335682} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3519f6d-17c0-5e34-805e-2f1cf9252804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48cd65ad-908c-58d2-aca2-a54d10434503" :question/math-academy-id "q-127011" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}]}
